  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows a 598 dam H5 high centered over the\nDesert Southwest with associated high amplitude ridging\nextending north all the way into northern Alberta and\nSaskatchewan. To the east of this, a mid/upper trough is in \nplace from Ontario southward into the Ohio Valley. Our CWA \nremains beneath northwesterly flow aloft between the western \nridge and the east/northeastern trough. Meanwhile, at the \nsurface, the weak cold front that moved through the region \nyesterday has stalled over the Ozarks, with a 1020 mb high \nsliding into eastern Nebraska. This has resulted in mostly clear\nskies and calm winds this morning, with temperatures in the \n60s. The synoptic scale pattern remains generally the same \nthroughout the day today. High temperatures this afternoon \nshould range from the lower 80s across far NE Missouri where the\nupper trough will have more influence, to as warm as the upper \n80s to 90 degrees for areas south and southwest of the KC metro \nwhere H5 heights will be higher closer to the western ridge. Dew\npoints should reside in the upper 50s to lower 60s, keeping \nconditions comfortable. By tomorrow, an upper trough is progged \nto come across the northern Pacific into British Columbia and \nthe Pacific Northwest, which will help push the upper ridge axis\na bit further east, with higher H5 heights creeping in from the\nwest. This should yield temperatures a couple degrees warmer \nfor Friday afternoon, but dew points in the upper 50s should \nkeep conditions relatively comfortable once again.\n\nThe upper trough across the PNW continues to push eastward into\nthe Intermountain West on Saturday and into the Northern High\nPlains by Sunday, with the H5 low deepening and an accompanying \nsurface cyclone developing. This will flatten the upper ridge \nand push the center of the H5 high over Texas and Oklahoma,\nsending temperatures into the low to mid 90s on Saturday and \nmid 90s on Sunday. This will also usher in increased southerly \nlow level flow, with south winds on the order of 15 to 20 mph \nand gusts up to 25 to 30 mph for Saturday and Sunday afternoons,\nand will yield modest moisture return, with dew points \nreturning to the low to mid 60s. The closed H5 low is progged to\nmove across southern Saskatchewan into central Manitoba on \nMonday and toward the Manitoba/Ontario border by Tuesday, \npushing a cold front across the Upper Midwest. Modest moisture \nreturn should continue, with dew points increasing to the mid \n60s by Monday afternoon and upper 60s by Tuesday afternoon. With\nincreased moisture and some weak forcing for ascent, chance \nPoPs returning to the forecast Monday morning (20 to 30%) and \nagain on Tuesday morning (20 to 40%), primarily for areas north \nof Interstate 70. Otherwise, expect hot and moderately humid \nconditions to continue for Monday through Wednesday, with highs \nin the mid 90s and max heat index values in the upper 90s to \nperhaps low 100s for most locations.",
